    <h1 id="about">MathEconLib</h1>
    <p class="tagline">Machine-checked foundations&nbsp;for new research in economics.</p>
    <p class="draft"><strong>Collaborator draft.</strong> Items in <span class="placeholder">amber italics</span> need confirming.</p>
    <p class="lede">MathEconLib is a long-term effort to build a shared formal foundation for economics in Lean 4. It is a public project founded by <a href="https://axiommath.ai">Axiom Math</a> and developed with economists at several universities. Its scope spans microeconomic theory, macroeconomic theory, and econometrics.</p>
    <h2 id="research">Use existing theorems to prove new ones</h2>
    <p>MathEconLib develops common definitions, explicit assumptions, and reusable intermediate results that researchers can combine and extend. Researchers can use these building blocks to assemble new models and develop results about preferences, incentives, equilibrium, dynamics, identification, estimation, and inference.</p>
    <p>Lean checks each new proof against the formalized foundations it uses. Shared assumptions make clear where a new model agrees with earlier formalized work, where it departs, and which differences open new research questions.</p>
    <p>MathEconLib builds on <a href="https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4">Mathlib</a> and encourages upstream contributions of broadly reusable mathematical results.</p>
    <p>MathEconLib is not an end-to-end autoformalization project. Its definitions and conventions are developed by economic theorists, with the aim of building a trusted foundation for formal research in economics.</p>
    <p>We welcome researchers to use AI to help with formalization and proof development, but researchers determine the mathematical architecture and Lean's kernel checks every theorem. Contributions receive human review for consistency with our <a href="https://github.com/AxiomMath/auctionlib/pull/77">draft design principles</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="vision">Long-term scope</h2>
    <p>MathEconLib is intended to cover all major mathematical areas of economics.</p>
    <dl class="scope">
      <dt>Microeconomic theory</dt>
      <dd>Preferences and choice, games, information, contracts, market and mechanism design, industrial organization, and general equilibrium.</dd>
      <dt>Macroeconomic theory</dt>
      <dd>Dynamic optimization, recursive methods, growth, heterogeneous-agent models, and monetary and fiscal theory.</dd>
      <dt>Econometrics</dt>
      <dd>Probability and statistical foundations, identification, estimation, inference, and causal and structural methods.</dd>
    </dl>

